const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Algorithm development","serviceType":"Data service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/algorithm-development-for-training-and-optimisation/","description":"Custom AI, machine learning, deep learning and optimisation algorithms for healthcare, life sciences and pharma.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Who uses this service?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Healthcare and life sciences researchers, data science teams, technology companies and organisations that need predictive analytics, automation or decision support inside a regulated or evidence-driven environment.\n\nThe common thread is that the output has to be defended to somebody — a reviewer, a regulator or a clinician — rather than only work."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What kinds of algorithm do you develop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning; predictive and prescriptive models; rules-based and heuristic algorithms; computer vision, natural language processing and speech recognition; and multi-objective optimisation.\n\nWhich one depends on the data, the constraint and whether the output has to be explainable. That last consideration eliminates more candidate approaches than anything else."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you establish that an algorithm actually works?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Against a baseline, on data it has not seen. We establish what the current approach achieves, then report the new model out of sample with preprocessing, feature selection and tuning all fitted inside the training folds.\n\nPerformance is reported by site, period and subgroup rather than only in aggregate, because an average conceals the place where it fails — and that is usually the finding that matters most."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does development take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Typically: requirement analysis and design, one to two weeks; development and initial testing, three to six weeks; optimisation and validation, two to four weeks; deployment and documentation, one to two weeks.\n\nThe variable that moves this most is data condition. A dataset that needs harmonising before anything can be trained adds more time than the modelling itself takes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you support the algorithm after deployment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is not optional if the model informs real decisions. Input distributions are monitored against the training distribution, performance is tracked where labels arrive, and retraining is triggered by a defined threshold.\n\nA model degrades as the population and the recording practices around it change. Nothing announces it — the outputs keep looking reasonable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is this different from software development?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Software executes rules you specify. An algorithm of this kind learns from data, which means its behaviour depends on data you did not write and may not fully understand.\n\nThe practical consequence is that the work is mostly validation. In conventional software you test whether it does what you specified; here you are establishing what it does at all, and on which populations."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you work in regulated clinical settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and the first question is whether what you are building is a regulated device. An algorithm that informs a clinical decision may be software as a medical device, which changes the evidence, the documentation and the change-control requirements substantially.\n\nThat classification is established at scoping. Discovering it after development is the most expensive way to find out."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/algorithm-development-for-training-and-optimisation/"}]}];

